            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">22</span></span>
          <math id="algorithm1.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}\leftarrow\mathcal{O}\cup\{(s,q,q_{1}),(s,q,q_{2}),\ldots,(s,q,q_{k})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒪</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒪</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em" mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}\leftarrow\mathcal{O}\cup\{(s,q,q_{1}),(s,q,q_{2}),\ldots,(s,q,q_{k})\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm1.4.24.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">;
</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.25" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.25.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">23</span></span>
          <span id="algorithm1.4.25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">return</span><span id="algorithm1.4.25.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="algorithm1.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm1.4.25.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">;
</span>
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now present our test oracle discovery algorithm, outlined in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The overall process consists of three main phases: database seeding, CAQ pairs generation, and equivalence checking. It leverages four key components as input: a target DBMS <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> DuckDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raasveldt and Mühleisen, 2019</a>)</cite>), an LLM <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> GPT o4-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite>), a SQL prover <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> SQLSolver <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2023</a>)</cite>), and a grammar-based generator <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> (<em id="S5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> SQLancer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba and Rigger, 2023</a>)</cite>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Database Seeding</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As equivalent CAQ pairs are associated with their database schemas, we first iterate <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> times to generate diverse schemas and their corresponding CAQs (line <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
In each iteration, we start by database seeding, which involves generating a random database schema (line <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), and then producing a CAQ that conforms to the generated schema, <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> the seed query <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> (line <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
Though existing grammar-based generators can be directly employed for schema and concrete query generation, the key challenge lies in producing those <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{PlaceholderEntry}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖯𝗅𝖺𝖼𝖾𝗁𝗈𝗅𝖽𝖾𝗋𝖤𝗇𝗍𝗋𝗒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{PlaceholderEntry}</annotation></semantics></math> in the seed CAQ while ensuring the syntactic correctness after instantiating them.
To achieve this, we force the generator to produce additional columns and tables in the schema, and then use those <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">virtual columns</em> and <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">virtual tables</em> to represent the two types of <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{PlaceholderEntry}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖯𝗅𝖺𝖼𝖾𝗁𝗈𝗅𝖽𝖾𝗋𝖤𝗇𝗍𝗋𝗒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{PlaceholderEntry}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Expression}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖤𝗑𝗉𝗋𝖾𝗌𝗌𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Expression}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Table}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖳𝖺𝖻𝗅𝖾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Table}</annotation></semantics></math>.
As shown in Listing <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, we create a virtual column <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">placeholder1</span> to represent an expression placeholder, <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> [<span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="text-decoration:line-through;text-decoration-color:#000000;;"><math id="S5.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square_{1}\mapsto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square_{1}\mapsto</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000080;text-decoration:line-through;text-decoration-color:#000000;;">t1:BOOLEAN</span>], and a virtual table <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">vtable1</span> for a table placeholder <em id="S5.SS1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> [<span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="text-decoration:line-through;text-decoration-color:#000000;;"><math id="S5.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square_{2}\mapsto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square_{2}\mapsto</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000080;text-decoration:line-through;text-decoration-color:#000000;;">TABLE</span>]. The generator, unaware of this abstraction, directly uses the concrete <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">placeholder1</span> and <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">vtable1</span>, effectively producing a valid CAQ.
This approach also allows our CAQ to be easily parsed by provers and understood by LLMs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="LST3" class="ltx_figure ltx_lstlisting ltx_align_center">
<div id="LST3.2" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_SQL ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_lstlisting ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing" style="--ltx-border-color:#000000;"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx31" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="lstnumberx31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">CREATE</span><span id="lstnumberx31.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx31.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">TABLE</span><span id="lstnumberx31.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx31.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t1</span><span id="lstnumberx31.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(</span><span id="lstnumberx31.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c0</span><span id="lstnumberx31.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx31.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keywords2 ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">INT</span><span id="lstnumberx31.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">,</span><span id="lstnumberx31.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx31.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">placeholder1 <span id="lstnumberx31.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BOOLEAN</span></span><span id="lstnumberx31.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">);</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx32" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="lstnumberx32.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">CREATE</span><span id="lstnumberx32.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx32.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">TABLE</span><span id="lstnumberx32.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx32.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t2</span><span id="lstnumberx32.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(</span><span id="lstnumberx32.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c0</span><span id="lstnumberx32.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx32.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keywords2 ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">INT</span><span id="lstnumberx32.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">);</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx33" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="lstnumberx33.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">CREATE</span><span id="lstnumberx33.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx33.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">TABLE</span><span id="lstnumberx33.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx33.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">vtable1(c0 <span id="lstnumberx33.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">INT</span>, c1 <span id="lstnumberx33.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">INT</span>)</span><span id="lstnumberx33.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">;</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx34" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="lstnumberx34.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">SELECT</span><span id="lstnumberx34.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx34.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[<span id="lstnumberx34.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="text-decoration:line-through;text-decoration-color:#000000;;"><math id="lstnumberx34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square_{1}\mapsto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square_{1}\mapsto</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="lstnumberx34.4.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000080;text-decoration:line-through;text-decoration-color:#000000;;">t1:BOOLEAN</span>]</span><span id="lstnumberx34.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx34.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000080;">t1.placeholder1</span><span id="lstnumberx34.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx34.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">FROM</span><span id="lstnumberx34.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx34.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t1</span><span id="lstnumberx34.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">,</span><span id="lstnumberx34.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx34.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">[<span id="lstnumberx34.13.1" class="ltx_text" style="text-decoration:line-through;text-decoration-color:#000000;;"><math id="lstnumberx34.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\square_{2}\mapsto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">□</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\square_{2}\mapsto</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="lstnumberx34.13.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000080;text-decoration:line-through;text-decoration-color:#000000;;">TABLE</span>]</span><span id="lstnumberx34.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx34.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000080;">vtable1</span><span id="lstnumberx34.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">;</span>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Listing 3: </span>An example of using concrete SQL query to represent CAQ when interfacing with grammar-based generators and provers.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2. </span>CAQ Pairs Generation</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining a seed CAQ <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and its schema <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, we proceed to generate a set of equivalent CAQs <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{q_{1},q_{2},\ldots,q_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{q_{1},q_{2},\ldots,q_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This task is handled by the <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{GenerateEqual}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖦𝖾𝗇𝖾𝗋𝖺𝗍𝖾𝖤𝗊𝗎𝖺𝗅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{GenerateEqual}</annotation></semantics></math> function (line <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> – <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), which iteratively prompts an LLM <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> to produce novel variants of <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. Our key strategy is to leverage in-context learning by providing the LLM with not only the seed query <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, but also carefully selected samples from two dynamically updated sets: <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Equal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖤𝗊𝗎𝖺𝗅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Equal}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> queries previously verified as equivalent to <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Fail}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖥𝖺𝗂𝗅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Fail}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> those that failed verification. The <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Equal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖤𝗊𝗎𝖺𝗅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Equal}</annotation></semantics></math> set promotes both correctness and diversity, while the <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Fail}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖥𝖺𝗂𝗅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Fail}</annotation></semantics></math> set provides examples of failures to avoid.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A crucial aspect of our approach is how we guide the LLM towards generating meaningful diversity. Instead of pursuing syntactic differences, our goal is to produce queries with varied <em id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">query plans</em>, as this is a key principle to creating effective test oracles in previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba and Rigger, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Rigger, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba and Rigger, 2024b</a>)</cite>. The prompt qualitatively asks the LLM to generate novel queries that are different from the provided examples.
We measure the similarity between query plans using the tree edit distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Shasha, 1989</a>)</cite>.
This quantitative value is then used to guide the selection of examples for the next iteration’s prompt.
Specifically, we use k-means to cluster the CAQs in the <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Equal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖤𝗊𝗎𝖺𝗅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Equal}</annotation></semantics></math> set based on the tree edit distance value and then sample queries from each cluster.
This strategy ensures the LLM is consistently shown a diverse range of successful query structures, pushing it to explore novel execution plans.
We defer the details of the clustering and sampling algorithms to <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Omitted Algorithm ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.
In summary, we can formalize the overall objective of the LLM’s generation task as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmlemma1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prob">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Problem 5.1 (Equivalent CAQ Generation).</h6>
<div id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a DBMS <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, a schema <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>, a seed CAQ <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, and a set <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Equal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖤𝗊𝗎𝖺𝗅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Equal}</annotation></semantics></math> of previously verified CAQs equivalent to <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, generate a new CAQ <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> such that (1) <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}\equiv q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≡</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}\equiv q</annotation></semantics></math> and (2) the difference between <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>’s query plan and the plans of queries in <math id="S5.Thmlemma1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Equal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖤𝗊𝗎𝖺𝗅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Equal}</annotation></semantics></math> is maximized.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">After the LLM generates a candidate query <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we employ a SQL equivalence decider <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> to formally verify its equivalence to the seed query <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}\vdash q\equiv q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>⊢</mo><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≡</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}\vdash q\equiv q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (line <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Additionally, we execute <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on the DBMS <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> to ensure it is syntactically correct and runs without errors, <em id="S5.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}\models q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⊧</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}\models q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. If both checks pass, <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is added to the <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Equal}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖤𝗊𝗎𝖺𝗅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Equal}</annotation></semantics></math> set (line <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> — <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>); otherwise, it goes into the <math id="S5.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Fail}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖥𝖺𝗂𝗅</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Fail}</annotation></semantics></math> set (line <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> — <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). This iterative process continues until a stopping criterion is met, such as reaching a maximum number of failed attempts (line <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) or hitting a predefined depth limit for iteration (line <a href="#algorithm1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3. </span>Equivalence Checking</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our framework, we use a SQL prover <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> to formally verify the equivalence between the seed CAQ <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and a generated candidate CAQ <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Since provers are designed for concrete SQL queries, we again use them to represent CAQs, as shown in Listing <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, <em id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> passing the schema with virtual columns and tables to the prover, and then determining the equivalence between the two concrete queries.
If the prover can formally prove that <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\equiv q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>≡</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\equiv q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, we say the two CAQs and their schema <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> form a valid test oracle, denoted as the triplet <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(S,q,q^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(S,q,q^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be used to test the target DBMS <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> in the following process.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Due to the hardness of the SQL equivalence problem, a formal prover is not guaranteed to verify every pair of equivalent queries. However, a positive result from the prover provides a definitive guarantee of equivalence. We leverage this property by treating the prover as a sound, but incomplete, verification tool. Our methodology deliberately retains only those CAQ pairs that are formally verified. This design choice prioritizes reliability, ensuring that our resulting test oracles are free from false positives and can be confidently used in the following testing phase.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Another limitation of those provers is that they typically support only a subset of SQL features.
To mitigate this, we first prove the equivalence between two CAQs, which typically contain fewer SQL features than concrete queries. After that we can instantiate those <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{PlaceholderEntry}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖯𝗅𝖺𝖼𝖾𝗁𝗈𝗅𝖽𝖾𝗋𝖤𝗇𝗍𝗋𝗒</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{PlaceholderEntry}</annotation></semantics></math> in the CAQs with more complex and feature-rich expressions and tables to create concrete queries for testing.
This highlights the effectiveness of <span id="S5.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>’s two-stage design, improving efficiency while also enriching the set of supported SQL features in the final test cases.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Test Cases Instantiation</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents our approach to instantiate test cases from the test oracles discovered in <a href="#S5" title="5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. As illustrated in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, our approach consists of three main steps: (1) synthesizing a corpus of SQL snippets, (2) instantiating test queries by populating placeholders in the test oracles with those generated in the first step, and (3) creating database instances to execute the instantiated test queries to detect bugs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm2" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span><math id="algorithm2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{TestCasesInstantiation}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖳𝖾𝗌𝗍𝖢𝖺𝗌𝖾𝗌𝖨𝗇𝗌𝗍𝖺𝗇𝗍𝗂𝖺𝗍𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{TestCasesInstantiation}</annotation></semantics></math></figcaption>
<div id="algorithm2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm2.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="algorithm2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"><span id="algorithm2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span><span id="algorithm2.4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
A DBMS <math id="algorithm2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, an LLM <math id="algorithm2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, a grammar-based generator <math id="algorithm2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, a set of test oracles <math id="algorithm2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}</annotation></semantics></math> generated in <a href="#S5" title="5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, and the number of test cases to be instantiated per test oracle <math id="algorithm2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"><span id="algorithm2.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> </span><span id="algorithm2.4.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">A set of bug reports </span><math id="algorithm2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm2.4.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.</span>
</div>
