<div id="algorithm2.4.24" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.24.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="algorithm2.4.24.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="algorithm2.4.24.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">   </span><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span><span id="algorithm2.4.24.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  </span>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.24.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">22</span></span>
          <math id="algorithm2.m39" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{B}\leftarrow\mathcal{B}\cup\{(s,\mathcal{D}\mathsf{.data},q^{*},q^{\prime*})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">s</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.900em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝖽𝖺𝗍𝖺</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><msup><mi mathsize="0.900em">q</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">′</mo><mo lspace="0.222em">⁣</mo><mo mathsize="0.900em">∗</mo></mrow></msup><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}\leftarrow\mathcal{B}\cup\{(s,\mathcal{D}\mathsf{.data},q^{*},q^{\prime*})\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm2.4.24.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> ;  </span><span id="algorithm2.4.24.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">// </span><a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3. Database Instantiation and Bug Report ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.25" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algorithm2.4.25.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">23</span></span>
          <span id="algorithm2.4.25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">return</span><span id="algorithm2.4.25.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="algorithm2.m40" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="algorithm2.4.25.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">;
</span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm2.4.26" class="ltx_listingline">
</div>
</div>
</figure>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.1. </span>Corpus Synthesis</h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The test case instantiation process begins with the synthesis of a SQL snippet corpus. This corpus provides a pool of expressions and tables used to populate the placeholders within our test oracles (line <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
To generate an effective and diverse corpus, we employ a hybrid strategy that leverages both an LLM <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> and a grammar-based generator <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. The motivation for this hybrid approach is: (1) LLMs excel at generating complex query structures and diverse database features, while (2) grammar-based generators can systematically cover a wide range of corner values and edge cases. As the grammar-based method follows established techniques, we focus this section on our LLM-based approach.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our LLM-based method starts with a default schema, which contains a single table incorporating all data types supported by the target DBMS:</p>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_SQL ltx_lstlisting ltx_listing" style="--ltx-border-color:#000000;"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,Q1JFQVRFIFRBQkxFIHQoYzEgSU5ULCBjMiBCT09MRUFOLCBjMyBURVhUIC4uLik7" download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx35" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="lstnumberx35.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">CREATE</span><span id="lstnumberx35.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx35.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">TABLE</span><span id="lstnumberx35.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx35.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t</span><span id="lstnumberx35.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(</span><span id="lstnumberx35.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c1</span><span id="lstnumberx35.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx35.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keywords2 ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">INT</span><span id="lstnumberx35.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">,</span><span id="lstnumberx35.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx35.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c2</span><span id="lstnumberx35.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx35.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">BOOLEAN</span><span id="lstnumberx35.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">,</span><span id="lstnumberx35.16" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx35.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c3</span><span id="lstnumberx35.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx35.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">TEXT</span><span id="lstnumberx35.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx35.21" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">...);</span>
</div>
</div>
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Upon this schema, we prompt <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate SQL expressions resulting in diverse datatype from the default table.
These expressions snippets can be reused across different test oracles by substituting the table and column names properly, which will be detailed in <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2. Query Instantiation ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>.
Furthermore, to guide <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> with the target DBMS <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>’s features, we employ a simple yet effective <em id="S6.SS1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Documentation-augmentation Generation</em> method:
We first collect the official documentation of the target DBMS and partition it into smaller pieces for each feature.
Then we sample a small set of feature documentation and prompt <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate SQL snippets with the sampled features.
This approach ensures that the generated SQL snippets are diverse enough to cover a wide range of features.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that, for table snippets, we cannot reuse them across different test oracles but only those shared the same schema <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.
This is mainly because table snippets must match the exact schema of the source tables they reference—including the specific table names, column names, and their datatypes. For instance, a table snippet that performs <span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">JOIN t1</span>, <span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">t2</span> can only be applied to test oracles whose schema <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> contains both tables <span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">t1</span> and <span id="S6.SS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">t2</span> with compatible structures.
Nevertheless, we can still leverage <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> to generate table snippets for each schema <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> without much overhead, as the number of unique schemas is usually small in practice.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">After the LLM generated snippets, we need to validate further whether they produce the expected datatype (<em id="S6.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e,</em> for expressions) or schema (<em id="S6.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e,</em> for tables) that we desire.
To this end, we employ a runtime validation step by executing the generated snippets on the target DBMS <math id="S6.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and checking their actual output types or schemas.
For example, as shown in Listing <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, we can directly apply those <span id="S6.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SELECT</span> statements and then check the metadata of the results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="LST4" class="ltx_figure ltx_lstlisting">
<div id="LST4.2" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_SQL ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_lstlisting ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing" style="--ltx-border-color:#000000;"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,U0VMRUNUIHtnZW5lcmF0ZWRfZXhwcmVzc2lvbn0gRlJPTSB0OyAtLSBkYXRhdHlwZQpTRUxFQ1QgKiBGUk9NIHtnZW5lcmF0ZWRfdGFibGV9OyAtLSBzY2hlbWEg" download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx36" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="lstnumberx36.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">SELECT</span><span id="lstnumberx36.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx36.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{</span><span id="lstnumberx36.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">generated_expression</span><span id="lstnumberx36.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">}</span><span id="lstnumberx36.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx36.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">FROM</span><span id="lstnumberx36.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx36.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t</span><span id="lstnumberx36.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">;</span><span id="lstnumberx36.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx36.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_comment ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">--<span id="lstnumberx36.13.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>datatype</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx37" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="lstnumberx37.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">SELECT</span><span id="lstnumberx37.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx37.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">*</span><span id="lstnumberx37.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx37.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">FROM</span><span id="lstnumberx37.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx37.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{</span><span id="lstnumberx37.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">generated_table</span><span id="lstnumberx37.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">};</span><span id="lstnumberx37.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx37.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_comment ltx_font_typewriter" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">--<span id="lstnumberx37.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space"> </span>schema</span>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Listing 4: </span>An example of runtime validating LLM-generated snippets in DuckDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raasveldt and Mühleisen, 2019</a>)</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If the execution fails, we discard the snippet; otherwise, we record its actual output type or schema for later use in <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2. Query Instantiation ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, to further generate more complex expressions, we introduce a technique we term <em id="S6.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cross-combination</em>. The key idea is to substitute a column reference in one expression with another expression, provided their data types are compatible.
For instance, as shown in Listing <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, consider a boolean expression <span id="S6.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">t.c1 + t.c1 &gt; 100</span> and an integer expression <span id="S6.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bit_count(t.c2)</span>, where <span id="S6.SS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">t.c2</span> is a boolean column. Because the first expression evaluates to a boolean, it can replace <span id="S6.SS1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">t.c2</span> in the second. This substitution yields a new, valid composite expression: <span id="S6.SS1.p6.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">bit_count(t.c1 + t.c1 &gt; 100)</span>. This recursive process allows us to build intricate expressions from simpler, generated snippets, thereby enriching our corpus and combining the strengths of LLM and grammar-based methods.</p>
</div>
<figure id="LST5" class="ltx_figure ltx_lstlisting ltx_align_center">
<div id="LST5.2" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_SQL ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_lstlisting ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing" style="--ltx-border-color:#000000;"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,KCokXG1hdGhzZntFeHByZXNzaW9ufVwgMSQqKTogKHQuYzEgKyB0LmMxID4gMTAwKSAoKiRcbWFwc3RvJCopIEJPT0xFQU4KKCokXG1hdGhzZntFeHByZXNzaW9ufVwgMiQqKTogYml0X2NvdW50KHQuYzIpICgqJFxtYXBzdG8kKikgSU5UCigqJFxSaWdodGFycm93JCopIGJpdF9jb3VudCh0LmMxICsgdC5jMSA+IDEwMCkgKCokXG1hcHN0byQqKSBJTlQ=" download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx38" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="lstnumberx38.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"><math id="lstnumberx38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Expression}\ 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖤𝗑𝗉𝗋𝖾𝗌𝗌𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Expression}\ 1</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="lstnumberx38.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">:</span><span id="lstnumberx38.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx38.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(</span><span id="lstnumberx38.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t</span><span id="lstnumberx38.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx38.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c1</span><span id="lstnumberx38.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx38.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">+</span><span id="lstnumberx38.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx38.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t</span><span id="lstnumberx38.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx38.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c1</span><span id="lstnumberx38.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx38.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&gt;</span><span id="lstnumberx38.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx38.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">100)</span><span id="lstnumberx38.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx38.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"><math id="lstnumberx38.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mapsto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mapsto</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="lstnumberx38.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx38.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">BOOLEAN</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx39" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="lstnumberx39.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"><math id="lstnumberx39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Expression}\ 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝖤𝗑𝗉𝗋𝖾𝗌𝗌𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Expression}\ 2</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="lstnumberx39.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">:</span><span id="lstnumberx39.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx39.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">bit_count</span><span id="lstnumberx39.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(</span><span id="lstnumberx39.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t</span><span id="lstnumberx39.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx39.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c2</span><span id="lstnumberx39.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">)</span><span id="lstnumberx39.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx39.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"><math id="lstnumberx39.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mapsto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mapsto</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="lstnumberx39.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx39.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keywords2 ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">INT</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx40" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="lstnumberx40.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"><math id="lstnumberx40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="lstnumberx40.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx40.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">bit_count</span><span id="lstnumberx40.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(</span><span id="lstnumberx40.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t</span><span id="lstnumberx40.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx40.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c1</span><span id="lstnumberx40.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx40.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">+</span><span id="lstnumberx40.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx40.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t</span><span id="lstnumberx40.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx40.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c1</span><span id="lstnumberx40.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx40.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&gt;</span><span id="lstnumberx40.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx40.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">100)</span><span id="lstnumberx40.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx40.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"><math id="lstnumberx40.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mapsto" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↦</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mapsto</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="lstnumberx40.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx40.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keywords2 ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">INT</span>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Listing 5: </span>An example of cross-combining expressions.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2. </span>Query Instantiation</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After synthesizing a corpus of SQL snippets, we proceed to instantiate the test queries by populating the placeholders in the test oracles with concrete expressions and tables from the generated corpus.
We detail this process in Algorithm <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>:
given a test oracle <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o=(s,q,q^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>q</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o=(s,q,q^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>, we iterate through each placeholder <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> in both queries <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (line <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Depending on whether the placeholder represents an expression or a table, we handle them differently.
For an expression placeholder, we first determine its expected datatype <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> and the table <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> its constraint belongs to.
Then, we randomly sample an expression <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> from the corpus <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> that matches the expected datatype <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> (line <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Since the columns in the sampled expression <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> refer to the default table, we need to replace them with columns from the target table <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> that have compatible datatypes.
We achieve this by iterating through each column in <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> (line <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), determining its datatype, and substituting it with a randomly sampled column from table <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> that matches the datatype (line <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For a table placeholder, we first extract its expected schema <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, we randomly sample a table <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> from the corpus <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> that matches the expected schema <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>s</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and is derived from the source schema <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> (line <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
Specifically, there are three ways to replace a table placeholder: <em id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> direct replacement, using CTE, or <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CREATE VIEW</span>, which will be randomly chosen.
Finally, we replace the placeholder <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> in both queries <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with the instantiated expression or table <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> (line <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
By following this procedure, we ensure that the instantiated queries <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>′</mo><mo lspace="0.222em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime*}</annotation></semantics></math> are syntactically valid and semantically equivalent, ready for execution on the target DBMS.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that, in <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3. Equivalence Checking ‣ 5. Test Oracle Discovery ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>, we prove the equivalence of the original queries <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> under the semantics of virtual columns and tables.
However, this equivalence is only guaranteed when those virtual columns and tables exist; it can be broken when the queries are instantiated with concrete expressions and tables that introduce semantic misalignments.
Therefore, to ensure that the instantiated queries <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>′</mo><mo lspace="0.222em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime*}</annotation></semantics></math> remain equivalent, we need to force the sampled snippets satisfying the following general constraints <math id="S6.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<ol id="S6.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Determinism</span> (<math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Table}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖳𝖺𝖻𝗅𝖾</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Table}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Expression}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖤𝗑𝗉𝗋𝖾𝗌𝗌𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Expression}</annotation></semantics></math>): The snippet should not contain any non-deterministic functions, such as <span id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RANDOM()</span>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Null-preserving</span> (<math id="S6.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Expression}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖤𝗑𝗉𝗋𝖾𝗌𝗌𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Expression}</annotation></semantics></math>): The expression should preserve a null return value when evaluated on rows only containing null values. For example, <span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">c1 + c2</span> is null-preserving, while <span id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">IFNULL(c1, 0)</span> is not.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Empty-preserving</span> (<math id="S6.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{Expression}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖤𝗑𝗉𝗋𝖾𝗌𝗌𝗂𝗈𝗇</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{Expression}</annotation></semantics></math>): The expression should return an empty result set when evaluated on an empty table. For example, <span id="S6.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sum(c1)</span> is not empty-preserving.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S6.SS2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">Specifically, the first constraint is also widely-used in previous works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020b</a>)</cite> and can be checked by applying a regular expression to identify non-deterministic features.
The second and third constraints resolve the semantic misalignment that arises in equivalence proofs involving virtual columns and tables. This misalignment occurs because a virtual column, intended to represent an expression, may behave differently than the expression itself under certain SQL semantics.
Furthermore, these constraints undergo runtime validation. A detailed analysis of counterexamples for violated constraints, along with the corresponding correctness proof for cases that satisfy the constraints, can be found in the Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Counter-Examples and Proofs ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3. </span>Database Instantiation and Bug Report</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After instantiating the test queries, we need to create database instances to execute them (line <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
We basically follow the widely-used random data generation method <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020b</a>)</cite> to populate the tables in the instantiated schema <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> with random tuples, and then create random indices on those tables to diversify the execution plans.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we execute the instantiated queries <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{\prime*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo>′</mo><mo lspace="0.222em">⁣</mo><mo>∗</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{\prime*}</annotation></semantics></math> on the target DBMS <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> and compare their results (line <a href="#algorithm2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>).
If the results differ, we report a logic bug along with the instantiated test case, database schema, and table data.
In addition, if a query causes a crash of <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, we also report a crash bug.
Note that, though our test oracles can also detect performance issues similar to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jung et al., 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022b</a>)</cite>, we do not detect them in a large scale due to those issues are typically regarded as expected behaviors by developers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba and Rigger, 2024a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7. </span>Evaluation</h2>

<figure id="S7.T1" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S7.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:93.1pt;vertical-align:-43.8pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(14.7pt,-4.0pt) scale(1.09301001908448,1.09301001908448) ;">
<table id="S7.T1.2.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S7.T1.2.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S7.T1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" rowspan="2"><span id="S7.T1.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">DBMS</span></th>
<th id="S7.T1.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t">
<span id="S7.T1.2.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:76.8pt;">
