<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we evaluate <span id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> in the following five aspects:</p>
<ol id="S7.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How many new bugs can <span id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> find in real-world DBMSs, and what is the contribution of its different test oracles to these findings? (<a href="#S7.SS1" title="7.1. New Bugs and Oracles ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.1</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How does <span id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> compare to baseline tools in terms of achieving coverages? (<a href="#S7.SS2" title="7.2. Code Coverage ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.2</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How does the number of automatically discovered oracles affect the quantity of unique bugs found, compared to a fixed set of oracles from prior work? (<a href="#S7.SS3" title="7.3. Effect of Test Oracles ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.3</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">How effective is the SQL equivalence prover in filtering out false positives? (<a href="#S7.SS4" title="7.4. Effect of SQL Equivalence Prover ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.4</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">What are the time and monetary costs required for <span id="S7.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> to generate a given number of test cases? (<a href="#S7.SS5" title="7.5. Cost and Efficiency Analysis ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.5</span></a>)</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Testbed</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conducted all experiments using a machine with 64 cores and 128 GB memory running on Ubuntu 24.04.
We use o4-mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025</a>)</cite> for all LLM calls with an Azure OpenAI API service in our experiments.
We evaluate <span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> on five widely-used DBMSs: Dolt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dolt, 2025</a>)</cite>, DuckDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raasveldt and Mühleisen, 2019</a>)</cite>, MySQL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">MySQL, 2025</a>)</cite>, PostgreSQL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Momjian, 2001</a>)</cite>, and TiDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
Note that, we only choose DBMSs that are actively maintained and have been comprehensively tested by at least one prior work, making new bug findings more valuable.
The details of the information of the target DBMS are shown in <a href="#S7.T1" title="In 7. Evaluation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.1. </span>New Bugs and Oracles</h3>

<section id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Bug report policy</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We continuously ran <span id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> on the five DBMSs for a three-month testing campaign.
We used the latest development versions and reported bugs only when they could be reproduced on their latest versions.
To avoid rediscovering known bugs, we first carefully reviewed the open issues of the target DBMS and our previous reports. Whenever a new patch was released for one of our reports, we updated the DBMS version and continued testing on the latest release.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Overall result</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S7.T2" title="In Overall result ‣ 7.1. New Bugs and Oracles ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> summarizes the statistics of the 40 bugs that <span id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> discovered during our testing campaign.
In total, <span id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> detected 40 previously unknown bugs, of which 35 are logic bugs that cause incorrect query results.
We also detected 5 other types of bugs, including crashes and performance issues, which are not the main focus of this paper, but also highlight the effectiveness of our corpus synthesis to generate complex, concrete queries.
Among the reports, 36 bugs have been confirmed by the developers, and 26 have already been fixed, and the remaining 2 bugs are duplicates: after developers reproduced and analyzed them, they found that these bugs shared the same root cause as some previously unfixed bugs.
Note that the number of logic bugs (<em id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> 35) underscores the effectiveness of our LLM-generated test oracles.
Compared with recent studies employing human carefully designed test oracles, <em id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> reporting 21 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba and Rigger, 2024b</a>)</cite>, 24 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Rigger, 2025</a>)</cite>, and 35 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang and Su, 2024</a>)</cite> logic bugs, our results remain noteworthy, particularly given that most of <span id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> ’s testing was conducted on DBMSs already examined by prior work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we show several representative bugs discovered by <span id="S7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> in our new test oracles.</p>
</div>
