</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.2. </span>Code Coverage</h3>

<div id="S7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare <span id="S7.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> with two mature DBMS logic bugs finding tools, SQLancer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba and Rigger, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020c</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Rigger, 2025</a>)</cite> and SQLancer++ <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong and Rigger, 2025a</a>)</cite> on DuckDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raasveldt and Mühleisen, 2019</a>)</cite> and PostgreSQL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Momjian, 2001</a>)</cite> in multiple coverage metrics.
As a state-of-the-art, open-source DBMS testing framework, SQLancer supports most of the latest test oracles <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba and Rigger, 2024a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang and Rigger, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
We compared with the latest version of SQLancer.
Meanwhile, SQLancer++ is a scalable variant that can be easily extended to multiple DBMSs with only lightweight modifications.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In terms of test oracles, <em id="S7.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">code coverage</em> indicates that the number of features and execution plans can be supported by them. Although code coverage is not strongly correlated with the ability to find logic bugs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong and Rigger, 2025a</a>)</cite>, it is still a fair metric to evaluate the diversity of test cases generated by <span id="S7.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> and the effectiveness of its corpus synthesis.
Meanwhile, <em id="S7.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">metamorphic coverage</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba et al., 2025</a>)</cite> is a more relevant metric for evaluating the effectiveness of test oracles and is highly related to logic bug finding abilities according to the historical bug study in SQLite and DuckDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
That is, given a pair of equivalent queries <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(Q_{1},Q_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(Q_{1},Q_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>, metamorphic coverage only counts the lines of code executed by solely <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S7.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> in the coverage information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S7.F3" title="In Examples (4) ‣ 7.1. New Bugs and Oracles ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the line, and branch code coverage for DuckDB and PostgreSQL from a 24-hour testing.
In DuckDB, <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> achieves 19.9% and 18.1% higher line and branch coverage than SQLancer++, respectively, and 11.3% and 3.4% higher than SQLancer.
In PostgreSQL, our tool outperforms SQLancer++ but slightly underperforms SQLancer.
This is expected, as SQLancer has been extensively optimized for PostgreSQL by the open-source community over many years.
Specifically, SQLancer covers 22 types of DDL and DML statements (<em id="S7.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CREATE SEQUENCE</span>) in PostgreSQL. In contrast, our tool currently focuses on detecting logic bugs within SQL queries.
Overall, the results demonstrate that <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> can generate diverse and complex test cases at scale, covering more code paths than state-of-the-art techniques in DuckDB. This indicates that the use of LLMs in <span id="S7.SS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> does not compromise its testing efficiency.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For metamorphic coverage, we compared <span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> with SQLancer on DuckDB across a fixed number of test cases, following the same setting in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
Note that, we did not evaluate metamorphic coverage on PostgreSQL, which has not been supported by  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba et al., 2025</a>)</cite>’s implementation.
Followed by the experimental setup in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, we generated 10 test suites for SQLancer, each with 100 test cases.
Specifically, SQLancer produce 50% of the test cases from TLP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020b</a>)</cite> and 50% from NoREC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020a</a>)</cite>.
For <span id="S7.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>, we generated 10 CAQ pairs as test suites, and instantiated each pair with 100 different snippets, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We reported the average metamorphic coverage of the 10 test suites in <a href="#S7.T3" title="In 7.2. Code Coverage ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
As shown, <span id="S7.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> achieves 5.473<math id="S7.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>, 6.431<math id="S7.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math>, and 5.571<math id="S7.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> higher line, function, and branch coverage than SQLancer, respectively.
We believe this is because <span id="S7.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>’s new test oracles can significantly alter the query structures between equivalent queries, thanks to LLM’s creativity, which covers a broader range of different code paths than prior works.
This significant improvement demonstrates the effectiveness of <span id="S7.SS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>’s new test oracles in comparing different execution paths and the potential to detect more logic bugs that SQLancer missed.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T3" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S7.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:258.8pt;height:58.4pt;vertical-align:-26.2pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(20.4pt,-4.6pt) scale(1.18753172364878,1.18753172364878) ;">
