</section>
<section id="S7.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.3. </span>Effect of Test Oracles</h3>

<figure id="S7.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06663v1/bisect.svg" id="S7.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/283;" width="429" height="283"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4. </span>Number of unique logic bugs found by different sets of oracles within a 6-hour testing on Dolt.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection evaluates <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>’s new oracles by comparing the number of unique logic bugs they detected in Dolt v1.0.0. We selected this historical version to ensure a fair comparison, as <span id="S7.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">git-bisect</span> allows us to precisely link each bug to its corresponding fix commit, providing an accurate count of unique bugs per method.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compared a composite baseline of test oracles against two sets automatically generated by <span id="S7.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>, with sizes of 50 and 5,000, <em id="S7.SS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> <span id="S7.SS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>-50 and <span id="S7.SS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>-5,000, respectively.
The baseline combines 11 test oracles from four previous works: TLP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020b</a>)</cite>, NoREC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020a</a>)</cite>, EET <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang and Su, 2024</a>)</cite>, and DQP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba and Rigger, 2024b</a>)</cite>.
To minimize confounding factors, we standardized the experimental conditions.
Specifically, the baseline oracles were represented in the same CAQ pair format and instantiated using the same snippet corpus as <span id="S7.SS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>. Furthermore, since the CAQ format requires an associated schema, we also randomly generated a new schema for the baseline oracles before each instantiation.
The details about how to convert the baseline oracles to CAQ pairs are provided in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Representing Existing Oracles ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S7.F4" title="In 7.3. Effect of Test Oracles ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the number of unique logic bugs found by different sets of oracles within a 6-hour testing.
We select a 6-hour time window for a fair comparison, following common practice in fuzz testing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Klees et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and prior studies on DBMS logic bug detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong and Rigger, 2025b</a>)</cite>, which typically fix the testing duration within a range of 1–24 hours.
As shown, <span id="S7.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>-5,000 found 10 unique bugs, significantly outperforming the baseline, which found only 3.
In contrast, <span id="S7.SS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>-50 found just 2 bugs.
This underperformance was expected, as the baseline oracles were carefully designed by human experts.
These results highlight the importance of oracle quantity in detecting unique logic bugs and underscore the limitations of manual oracle design, thus highlighting the necessity of <span id="S7.SS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>’s automated test oracle discovery.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.4. </span>Effect of SQL Equivalence Prover</h3>

<div id="S7.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate our SQL equivalence prover’s false positive and false-negative rates against an LLM-as-a-judge baseline. For this baseline, we use GPT-5 to determine whether a candidate CAQ pair is equivalent directly.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">False positive bug reports</h4>

<div id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify the prover’s impact on false positives, we performed an ablation that disables the prover while using an LLM-as-a-judge approach <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and ran <span id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> on DuckDB until collecting 20 bug reports.
We then manually adjudicated each report as a true positive or a false positive.
Surprisingly, we found that <span id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">all</span> of them were false positives.
For instance, LLM-as-a-judge can incorrectly judge the two queries shown in Listing <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> as equivalent, which is actually non-equivalent when <math id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v.c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">.</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v.c</annotation></semantics></math> is <span id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NULL</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="LST11" class="ltx_figure ltx_lstlisting">
<div id="LST11.2" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_SQL ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_lstlisting ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing" style="--ltx-border-color:#000000;"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,U0VMRUNUICogRlJPTSB2IFdIRVJFIFRSVUU7ClNFTEVDVCAqIEZST00gdiBXSEVSRSB2LmMgPj0gdi5jIE9SIHYuYyA8IHYuYw==" download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx90" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <span id="lstnumberx90.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">SELECT</span><span id="lstnumberx90.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx90.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">*</span><span id="lstnumberx90.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx90.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">FROM</span><span id="lstnumberx90.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx90.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">v</span><span id="lstnumberx90.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx90.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">WHERE</span><span id="lstnumberx90.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx90.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">TRUE</span><span id="lstnumberx90.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">;</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx91" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2</span>
                
                
                
                
                
                
                
              <span id="lstnumberx91.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">SELECT</span><span id="lstnumberx91.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx91.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">*</span><span id="lstnumberx91.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx91.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">FROM</span><span id="lstnumberx91.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx91.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">v</span><span id="lstnumberx91.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx91.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">WHERE</span><span id="lstnumberx91.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx91.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">v</span><span id="lstnumberx91.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx91.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c</span><span id="lstnumberx91.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx91.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&gt;=</span><span id="lstnumberx91.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx91.18" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">v</span><span id="lstnumberx91.19" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx91.20" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c</span><span id="lstnumberx91.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx91.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">OR</span><span id="lstnumberx91.23" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx91.24" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">v</span><span id="lstnumberx91.25" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx91.26" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c</span><span id="lstnumberx91.27" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx91.28" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;</span><span id="lstnumberx91.29" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx91.30" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">v</span><span id="lstnumberx91.31" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx91.32" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c</span>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Listing 11: </span>A false positive example from LLM-as-a-judge.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To further investigate the issue of extremely high false positive rates, we generated 20 candidate CAQ pairs that the LLM judged as equivalent, and manually verified them.
We found that 1 of these 20 pairs was an incorrect test oracle, <em id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> non-equivalent CAQ pairs.
While the accuracy of LLM-as-a-judge is high, bugs in mature DBMSs are exceedingly rare, <em id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> one bug against thousands of queries.
Consequently, even a small false positive rate can generate a volume of false reports that overwhelmingly drown out true positives.
In addition, such false positives are particularly unacceptable when <span id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> is integrated into CI/CD pipelines or production environments, as developers have limited time to investigate each report.
Therefore, the SQL equivalence prover is necessary for ensuring the soundness of <span id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>’s test oracles and making it practical for real-world use.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">False negatives in test-oracle generation</h4>

<div id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To estimate the prover’s false-negative rate, <em id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> unable to prove correct test oracles, we ran two configurations of <span id="S7.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> on DuckDB: one using the prover to check CAQ pairs and one using LLM-as-a-judge.
In each configuration, we continued until the mechanism had rejected 10 candidate CAQ pairs as “non-equivalent” then manually adjudicated each pair.
We found that 8 of the 10 pairs were false negatives for the prover, while 5 of the 10 pairs were false negatives for the LLM-as-a-judge.
The results also show that the prover’s false negative rate is comparable to that of state-of-the-art LLMs.
Note that, such false negatives from the prover are also expected, as it is intentionally conservative to ensure soundness.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">7.5. </span>Cost and Efficiency Analysis</h3>

<div id="S7.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now evaluate the impact of CAQ pairs on cost and efficiency by comparing <span id="S7.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> against the naive baseline introduced in <a href="#S1" title="1. Introduction ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. This baseline uses the same SQL equivalence prover but directly prompts an LLM to generate complete equivalent pairs from seed queries, rather than CAQ pairs.
When tested on Dolt 1.0.0 for one hour, <span id="S7.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> detected a logic bug while the baseline found none, and we measured the corresponding test cases generated and LLM costs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.06663v1/throughput.svg" id="S7.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/255;" width="429" height="255"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5. </span>Cost and efficiency comparison between <span id="S7.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> and the naive baseline on Dolt over a 1-hour run.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S7.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <a href="#S7.F5" title="In 7.5. Cost and Efficiency Analysis ‣ 7. Evaluation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <span id="S7.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> achieves a significantly higher test case throughput than the naive baseline. Unlike the baseline, which slowly generates complete equivalent pairs, <span id="S7.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> spends only a few minutes creating abstract CAQ pairs (Phase I) and then rapidly instantiates them (Phase II).
This process is also highly economical: generating the CAQ pairs costs about $3 in LLM calls, and instantiating test cases costs roughly $1 per 1,000, leveraging a reusable corpus of 100,000 snippets built for only $12. In summary, <span id="S7.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> is a cost-effective solution for detecting logic bugs with a high throughput.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8. </span>Discussion</h2>

<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Additional finding</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to improving DBMS robustness, we also discovered several bugs in existing SQL equivalence provers, which can introduce inherent false positives that impede our testing campaign.
For example, in an early version of SQLSolver <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, it incorrectly proved the two queries in Listing <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> to be equivalent: the first query always returns <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">false</span> when <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">t1</span> is non-empty, whereas the second query can return <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">true</span> when both <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">c0</span> and <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">c1</span> are <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">false</span>, and even <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NULL</span> when either <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">c0</span> or <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">c1</span> is <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NULL</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="LST12" class="ltx_figure ltx_lstlisting ltx_align_center">
<div id="LST12.2" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_SQL ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_lstlisting ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing" style="--ltx-border-color:#000000;"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64,Q1JFQVRFIFRBQkxFIHQxKGMwIEJPT0xFQU4sIGMxIEJPT0xFQU4pOwpTRUxFQ1QgZmFsc2UgRlJPTSB0MTsKU0VMRUNUICh0MS5jMSA8IHQxLmMwKSA8ICh0MS5jMSA9IHQxLmMxKSBGUk9NIHQxOw==" download="">⬇</a></div>
<div id="lstnumberx92" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="lstnumberx92.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">CREATE</span><span id="lstnumberx92.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx92.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">TABLE</span><span id="lstnumberx92.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx92.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t1</span><span id="lstnumberx92.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(</span><span id="lstnumberx92.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c0</span><span id="lstnumberx92.9" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx92.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">BOOLEAN</span><span id="lstnumberx92.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">,</span><span id="lstnumberx92.12" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx92.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c1</span><span id="lstnumberx92.14" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx92.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">BOOLEAN</span><span id="lstnumberx92.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">);</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx93" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="lstnumberx93.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">SELECT</span><span id="lstnumberx93.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx93.4" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">false</span><span id="lstnumberx93.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx93.6" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">FROM</span><span id="lstnumberx93.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx93.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t1</span><span id="lstnumberx93.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">;</span>
</div>
<div id="lstnumberx94" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="lstnumberx94.2" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">SELECT</span><span id="lstnumberx94.3" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx94.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(</span><span id="lstnumberx94.5" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t1</span><span id="lstnumberx94.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx94.7" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c1</span><span id="lstnumberx94.8" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx94.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;</span><span id="lstnumberx94.10" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx94.11" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t1</span><span id="lstnumberx94.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx94.13" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c0</span><span id="lstnumberx94.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">)</span><span id="lstnumberx94.15" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx94.16" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;</span><span id="lstnumberx94.17" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx94.18" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">(</span><span id="lstnumberx94.19" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t1</span><span id="lstnumberx94.20" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx94.21" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c1</span><span id="lstnumberx94.22" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx94.23" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">=</span><span id="lstnumberx94.24" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx94.25" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t1</span><span id="lstnumberx94.26" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">.</span><span id="lstnumberx94.27" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">c1</span><span id="lstnumberx94.28" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">)</span><span id="lstnumberx94.29" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx94.30" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_keyword ltx_font_typewriter ltx_font_bold">FROM</span><span id="lstnumberx94.31" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_space ltx_font_typewriter"> </span><span id="lstnumberx94.32" class="ltx_text ltx_lst_identifier ltx_font_typewriter">t1</span><span id="lstnumberx94.33" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">;</span>
</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Listing 12: </span>Incorrect equivalence proof in SQLSolver <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A root cause is that these provers are research prototypes and are typically tested only on small benchmarks due to the difficulty of constructing comprehensive test suites; for instance, Caclite test cases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Begoli et al., 2018</a>)</cite>, the benchmark used in  <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al., 2017a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, contains only about 200 equivalent query pairs.
Therefore, our work can also help improve the reliability of SQL equivalence provers.
In total, we reported 10 bugs in SQLSolver <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and QED <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, all of which have been fixed by the developers.
This also allowed us to continue our testing campaign without being blocked by such inherent false positives.
Note that, the soundness of our approach should be distinguished from the correctness of our implementation.
Those prover bugs, which we treat as components of our implementation, may lead to false positives in our testing results before fixing, but do not compromise the soundness of our approach.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations and future work</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach has three major limitations that present opportunities for future work.</p>
<ol id="S8.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S8.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our SQL equivalence prover currently supports only a subset of SQL features, limiting the scope of test oracles that <span id="S8.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> can generate.
For instance, even the state-of-the-art SQL equivalence prover, SQLSolver <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, does not understand the semantics of most functions, treating them as uninterpreted functions.
In addition, since all SQL equivalence provers are currently built upon Calcite <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Begoli et al., 2018</a>)</cite> grammar, SQL dialects may also pose challenges.
This limitation can be addressed by extending the prover to support more SQL features and combining SQL dialect translator <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, which is an active area of research.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> currently focuses on relational DBMS and SQL, but the underlying principles can be extended to other types of databases and query languages, such as graph DBMS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhuang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and spatial DBMS testing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deng et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S8.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S8.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> focuses on automatically discovering test oracles, but does not provide a mechanism to prioritize or rank the generated oracles. We believe that, after <span id="S8.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>, DBMS testing research can shift from manually crafting test oracles to developing techniques for prioritizing and selecting the most effective oracles from a large pool of candidates.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Broader impact</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior to <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>, using LLMs to generate test oracles was impractical because testing is sensitive to false positives, and it is infeasible to use test cases to validate LLM-generated oracles.
There is some initial work in discovering test oracles for simple programs without soundness guarantees <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.
However, to the best of our knowledge, <span id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> is the first to overcome this challenge in a practical setting, <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> DBMS testing, by combining LLMs with a sound SQL equivalence prover and making LLM-based test oracle generation practical.
This opens up exciting opportunities for future research to explore the use of LLMs in other testing domains, such as compilers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Le et al., 2014</a>)</cite> and SMT solvers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Winterer et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, where test oracles are also challenging to create.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9. </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM in system research</h4>

<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs have been applied to various system research tasks, including code generation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mündler et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia and Zhang, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patil et al., [n. d.]</a>)</cite>, automated tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Giannakouris and Trummer, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, data processing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zeighami et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Patel et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shankar et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, program analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, networking <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and algorithm discovery <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Novikov et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rybin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Among these, LLM-aided testing and LLM-for-DBMS are the most relevant to our work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S9.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S9.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In LLM-aided testing, prior work has primarily focused on generating effective inputs. For example, Fuzz4all <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xia et al., 2024</a>)</cite> is a universal fuzzing framework that leverages LLMs to generate test inputs for various applications, while other research applies LLMs to fuzzing in specific domains such as compilers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, kernels <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, and smart contracts <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Similarly, ShQvel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhong and Rigger, 2025b</a>)</cite> uses LLMs to generate feature-rich SQL queries for testing DBMS with manually crafted test oracles, while SQLStorm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schmidt et al., 2025</a>)</cite> employs LLMs to synthesize a large-scale benchmark for detecting crashes and evaluating performance of DBMSs.
While prior approaches generate complex test inputs to find crash bugs, our work focuses on generating test oracles to detect logic bugs, along with effective methods for their initialization. This contribution is therefore orthogonal and complementary to existing efforts.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S9.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In LLM-for-DBMS, prior work mainly focus on text-to-SQL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, query optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, dialect translation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, and hyperparameter tuning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Giannakouris and Trummer, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Among these works, LLM-R2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024b</a>)</cite> provides a framework to guide LLMs’ use of existing rules, <em id="S9.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.,</em> Calcite, to optimize SQL queries.
Though this method can also be applied to our task, it is impractical because of the high cost of rewriting concrete queries for testing and the difficulty of finding bugs by DBMS’s query rewriting rules, which are typically simple and well-tested.
To the best of our knowledge, our work is the first to leverage LLMs to generate test oracles for DBMSs, and we believe that our rule generation framework can also be applied to other DBMS tasks in the future, such as query optimization with performance improvement.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">DBMS testing and verification</h4>

<div id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There is a rich body of work on DBMS testing, verification, and their applications.</p>
<ol id="S9.I2" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S9.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior work in DBMS testing has largely followed two distinct research paths: <em id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">test query generation</em> for fuzzing and <em id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">test oracle design</em> for detecting silent bugs. The first path focuses on automatically generating complex SQL queries to find crashes, with techniques being either grammar-based, exemplified by tools like SQLsmith <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Seltenreich, 2022</a>)</cite> and SQLancer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020c</a>)</cite>, or mutation-based, as seen in Griffin <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. In parallel, the second path concentrates on test oracles for detecting “silent” bugs such as logic and performance issues, an area that has traditionally relied on significant manual effort. For instance, oracles like TLP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020b</a>)</cite>, NoREC <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rigger and Su, 2020a</a>)</cite>, and EET <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang and Su, 2024</a>)</cite> were designed for logic bugs, while others such as CERT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ba and Rigger, 2024a</a>)</cite> and Apollo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jung et al., 2019</a>)</cite> target performance issues. This manual crafting of oracles remains a primary bottleneck in fully automated DBMS testing. <span id="S9.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> is the first work that tackles this critical challenge by focusing on the automatic discovery of test oracles.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S9.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S9.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S9.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has focused on improving the effectiveness of SQL equivalence provers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al., 2017a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chu et al., 2017b</a>)</cite> and disprovers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">He et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. These tools have been widely applied in various DBMS scenarios, including query rewriting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, text-to-SQL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>, and user-database interaction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Purich et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Among these, Wetune <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2022</a>)</cite> is the most relevant to our work, as it employs a SQL equivalence prover to verify query rewriting rules generated via a brute-force search. However, its enumeration-based approach suffers from two key limitations. First, it can only discover simple rules due to the high time complexity of enumeration, <em id="S9.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.,</em> those with <math id="S9.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 4</annotation></semantics></math> nodes in the query plan. Second, because it lacks a placeholder mechanism as CAQ, the resulting rules support a very limited set of SQL features.
These restrictions render the approach impractical for testing modern DBMSs. In contrast, our LLM-based approach overcomes these shortcomings by generating complex rules and instantiating them with a diverse range of SQL snippets.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10. </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The manual construction of test oracles remains a major bottleneck in discovering logic bugs in modern DBMSs.
We present <span id="S10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>, the first automated framework to use LLMs for generating sound and effective test oracles. <span id="S10.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> achieves scalability through a novel CAQ abstraction and guarantees soundness with an SQL equivalence prover. In experiments on five extensively-tested DBMSs, <span id="S10.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span> discovered 40 previously unknown bugs, including 35 logic bugs. Its new test oracles improve both coverage and logic-bug detection beyond existing works.
We believe <span id="S10.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_sansserif">Argus</span>’s approach not only provides a solution to the long-standing bottleneck of fully automated DBMS testing but also opens new opportunities for testing other complex software domains with LLMs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S11" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">11. </span>Ackowledgements</h2>

<div id="S11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We thank Jinsheng Ba and Manuel Rigger for their insightful feedback, Haoran Ding, Sicheng Pan, and Shuxian Wang for their support on SQL equivalence solvers, and the DBMS developers for confirming and fixing the reported bugs.</p>
</div>
</section>
