</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Counter-Examples and Proofs</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide three counter-examples to demonstrate that the potential false positives if we do not apply the three general constraints of <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> mentioned in <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2. Query Instantiation ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>.
Specifically, Listing <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, Listing <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, and Listing <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> show three cases that can be formally proven in the scenario of virtual columns, but lead to false positives in the instantiation phase by missing the constraints.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.LST1" class="ltx_figure ltx_lstlisting">
<div id="A3.LST1.2" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_language_SQL ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_lstlisting ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle ltx_listing" style="--ltx-border-color:#000000;"><div class="ltx_listing_data"><a href="data:text/plain;base64," download="">⬇</a></div>
