<div id="A3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use these constraints to ensure that snippets replacing column expressions are deterministic, preserve null values during outer joins, and refer to a unique row’s column combination in the cases other than outer joins.
We then prove the following theorem to show that if we follow these constraints, the equivalence is preserved after instantiating expressions.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_smallcaps ltx_title_theorem">Theorem C.1 (Expression Instantiation Preserves Equivalence).</h6>
<div id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider two SQL queries, <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, that are <span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">semantically equivalent</span> on a schema <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> containing virtual columns <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> be the queries created by replacing every occurrence of each virtual column <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with its corresponding expression <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. If each expression <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> references a <span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unique row’s</span> column combination in all cases other than outer joins, then <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are also <span id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">semantically equivalent</span> on the schema <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is <math id="A3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> without the virtual columns.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">We prove this by contradiction. Assume that <math id="A3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> are not semantically equivalent on schema <math id="A3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, meaning there exists a database instance <math id="A3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="A3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>S</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})\neq Q_{2}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})\neq Q_{2}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>. We then construct a database <math id="A3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> on schema <math id="A3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> by augmenting <math id="A3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> with the virtual columns <math id="A3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{v_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{v_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> defined by their corresponding expressions <math id="A3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The core of this proof is establishing that for any query <math id="A3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, the results <math id="A3.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}(D)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> are identical. This equivalence is guaranteed by two properties of the expressions <math id="A3.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{e_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{e_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. First, the <span id="A3.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">unique row reference</span> property ensures that for any standard join or matched row, the value of a virtual column <math id="A3.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A3.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is computed from the same single row as its expression <math id="A3.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A3.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding an identical result. Second, and crucially for outer joins, the <span id="A3.p3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">null-preserving</span> property handles unmatched rows. If an outer join produces a row where the columns underlying an expression <math id="A3.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> are padded with <span id="A3.p3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NULL</span>, the expression <math id="A3.p3.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> itself evaluates to <span id="A3.p3.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NULL</span> in query <math id="A3.p3.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>. The null-preserving property explicitly forces the corresponding virtual column <math id="A3.p3.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> in query <math id="A3.p3.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to also resolve to <span id="A3.p3.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">NULL</span> in this exact scenario.
This perfect mirroring of behavior ensures that <math id="A3.p3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}(D)=Q_{1}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}(D)=Q_{1}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}(D)=Q_{2}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}(D)=Q_{2}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, since <math id="A3.p3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})\neq Q_{2}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>D</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})\neq Q_{2}^{\prime}(D^{\prime})</annotation></semantics></math> by our initial assumption, it follows that <math id="A3.p3.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}(D)\neq Q_{2}(D)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}(D)\neq Q_{2}(D)</annotation></semantics></math>, contradicting the premise that <math id="A3.p3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.p3.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are semantically equivalent on schema <math id="A3.p3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This theorem shows that, under the scenario of only virtual columns, the equivalence is preserved after instantiating expressions if we follow the constraints mentioned in <a href="#S6.SS2" title="6.2. Query Instantiation ‣ 6. Test Cases Instantiation ‣ Automated Discovery of Test Oracles for Database Management Systems Using LLMs" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a>.
When there are virtual tables, it is much easier to show the preserved equivalence after instantiating tables, since the tables are simply replaced by other tables with the same schema.
If there is no non-deterministic rows in the instantiated tables, the equivalence is also preserved after instantiating tables, because such instantiation is equivalent to using a <span id="A3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CREATE VIEW</span> to replace the <span id="A3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">CREATE TABLE</span> for virtual tables.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Note that, while these proofs do not cover all SQL features, especially for those new features in future SQL standards, we have not found any counterexamples in our experiments that violate the equivalence after instantiating expressions following these constraints.
In practice, we can maintain the soundness of instantiated queries by disabling any new features that invalidate existing proofs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</article>
</div>
</div>
